    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d389">

                <pb facs="0360" n="352" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg352"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102/90½</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-12">June 12, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>It was not intended to convey in my memorandum of June 11 any implication that we
                    should “stand in our tracks as regards diplomatic pursuit of our objectives in
                    China or fear even to discuss with the British and the French” etc.</p>
                <p>With regard to points 1 and 2 raised by the Chinese Government, the Department
                    has given and is continuing to give careful attention to developments; the
                    American Government has, as need therefor has arisen, made declarations and has
                    approached the British and the French Governments. I would assume that we would
                    still be prepared to follow those courses if occasion should arise. FE<anchor
                        n="63" xml:id="d389-f1"/> perceives no development at this moment which
                    would make useful a declaration such as the Chinese suggest or an approach to
                    the British and the French Governments.</p>
                <closer><signed>M[<hi rendition="#small-caps">axwell</hi>] M. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">amilton</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
